  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "A bit of a niche use case, but it would be nice if there were a means of verifying if a http request from a vrchat client came from the map it was intended from. Http requests could contain the wrld_id either as part of the user_agent or as a http get parameter.\n \nRecently I found out that one of my server end points that I use for a local time clock shader was used by someone else for their own map without permission. As my host charges on a per usage basis, I incurred additional cost as their map was fairly popular. How they got that url endpoint aside, it would be nice if there were some way of making sure your servers would only serve requests if they came from users in your own maps.\n\nA simple and effective way would be to put in the world id as part of the http request, either as a get parameter or part of the user agent string. The server can then validate that it did indeed came from a white listed world, and reject requests that did not.\n\nWhile the current clock shaders will go away with Udon, I still think this might have some valid uses for those who want to make sure others cannot use their remote assets without permission, and depending on the nature of them, such as large video files etc, bandwidth costs would be non-trivial.",
